## Static analysis and rule configuration

Stylelint checks stylesheets against rules to **catch errors and enforce conventions**. Error checks include invalid syntax or values, unknown identifiers, duplicates and missing constructs. Convention rules cover allowed/disallowed features and complexity limits. The rule index marks rules enabled by the standard configuration with ✅ and autofixable rules with 🔧. [Rules](https://stylelint.io/user-guide/rules/)

Configuration is an exported object containing a `rules` map. You can extend shared configurations, override their rules and load plugins. A rule’s primary option controls what it checks; secondary options control behavior such as severity. [Customizing](https://stylelint.io/user-guide/customize/) · [Configuring](https://stylelint.io/user-guide/configure/)

For example:

```json
{
  "rules": {
    "color-no-invalid-hex": [
      true,
      { "severity": "warning" }
    ]
  }
}
```

`severity: "warning"` lowers the diagnostic severity; it does **not** ignore the code. The default severity is `"error"`. Do not confuse numeric primary options—such as `selector-max-id: 0`, a selector limit—with severity settings. [Configuring](https://stylelint.io/user-guide/configure/) · [Customizing](https://stylelint.io/user-guide/customize/)

## Automatic fixing

Run:

```sh
stylelint "**/*.css" --fix
```

Only rules supporting autofix change code; other violations still require manual attention. Examples of fixable checks include deprecated syntax, unspaced `calc()` operators, vendor prefixes and unnecessary zero-length units. [Customizing](https://stylelint.io/user-guide/customize/) · [Rules](https://stylelint.io/user-guide/rules/)

The default **strict** fix mode only fixes code without syntax errors. **Lax** mode uses `postcss-safe-parser` to fix as much as possible despite syntax errors. A rule’s secondary option `disableFix: true` disables its fixes without disabling its checks. Legacy rules using deprecated `context.fix` have broader disable-comment restrictions: a relevant disable comment can prevent that rule’s fixes throughout the source. [Options](https://stylelint.io/user-guide/options/)

## Ignoring code and files

Inline comments suppress all rules or named rules:

```css
/* stylelint-disable declaration-no-important */
a { color: red !important; }
/* stylelint-enable declaration-no-important */

/* stylelint-disable-next-line declaration-no-important -- Exception */
b { color: blue !important; }
```

`stylelint-disable-line` suppresses the current line. Disabled regions also bypass applicable fixes. Reasons follow ` -- `, with spaces on both sides. [Ignoring code](https://stylelint.io/user-guide/ignore-code/)

For entire files, use `.stylelintignore` with `.gitignore` syntax, `ignoreFiles` in configuration, or CLI ignore options. `node_modules` is ignored by default. [Ignoring code](https://stylelint.io/user-guide/ignore-code/) · [CLI](https://stylelint.io/user-guide/cli/)

Audit exceptions using options that report needless, descriptionless, invalid-scope or unscoped disables. `--ignore-disables` instead checks code as though disable comments were absent. [Options](https://stylelint.io/user-guide/options/)

**Evidence limitation:** captured documentation did not establish detailed `ignoreFiles` resolution or suppressions-file semantics.

CS-SER-01-live-off-r1-20261103T162830887.text:
Migrating to flat config changes both the configuration’s structure and how files are ignored.

### 1. File names
Replace legacy `.eslintrc.*` configuration with **`eslint.config.js`**, `.mjs`, or `.cjs`, normally in the project root. TypeScript variants—`.ts`, `.mts`, and `.cts`—are also supported but require additional setup. Flat config has been ESLint’s default since v9.0.0. ([Configuration files](https://eslint.org/docs/latest/use/configure/configuration-files#configuration-file), [Migration guide](https://eslint.org/docs/latest/use/configure/migration-guide#start-using-flat-config-files))

### 2. Exported format
Instead of exporting one legacy configuration object, export an **array of configuration objects**. Each object can target files through `files` glob patterns; this replaces the legacy `overrides` structure. Documentation examples use `defineConfig()` and an ES-module default export; CommonJS configurations use `module.exports`. Rule-setting syntax itself remains unchanged. ([Configuration files](https://eslint.org/docs/latest/use/configure/configuration-files#configuration-file), [Migration guide](https://eslint.org/docs/latest/use/configure/migration-guide#glob-based-configs))

For example:

```js
// eslint.config.mjs
import { defineConfig } from "eslint/config";

export default defineConfig([
    { ignores: ["**/dist/**", "**/.*"] },
    {
        files: ["**/*.js"],
        rules: { semi: "error" }
    }
]);
```

### 3. Ignored-file behaviour
- **Move ignore handling into flat config.** ESLint’s migration guidance says flat config does not automatically load `.eslintignore`; migrate its patterns into the configuration. ([Migration guide](https://eslint.org/docs/latest/use/configure/migration-guide#ignoring-files))
- **Global and local ignores differ.** An object containing only `ignores` (optionally also `name`) excludes matches globally. Adding `ignores` alongside `rules` or other settings only excludes files from that particular object—not necessarily from linting altogether. Only global ignores can match directories directly. The `globalIgnores()` helper makes global intent explicit. ([Configuration files](https://eslint.org/docs/latest/use/configure/configuration-files#globally-ignore-files-with-ignores))
- **Review pattern scope.** Patterns normally resolve relative to the config file. A pattern such as `.config/` targets that location; use `**/.config/` for directories with that name anywhere. With an alternate `--config`, patterns resolve relative to the working directory. ([Ignore files](https://eslint.org/docs/latest/use/configure/ignore#glob-pattern-resolution))
- **Dotfiles are not generally ignored by default.** Add an explicit pattern such as `**/.*` if desired. Default ignores are `**/node_modules/` and `.git/`. ([Configuration files](https://eslint.org/docs/latest/use/configure/configuration-files#globally-ignore-files-with-ignores))

**Version caveat:** These are the current documentation’s conventions; TypeScript loading requirements and helper availability should be checked against your installed ESLint version.
